WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:06.040
This beautiful tuck lace stitch
pattern can be worked on any machine.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.200
Tuck lace is a combination of tuck
and needles out of work to create open,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.840
lace-like stitch patterns
without transferring stitches.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.560
We'll discuss manual,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.120
automatic patterning machines,
both punch card and electronics,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.280
and explore both Brother and Silver/Singer/
Studio machines in this video.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.480
Now, please don't skip the manual
machines demo at the beginning.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.680
It'll go a long way towards your

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.600
understanding how this
stitch pattern is created.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.440
Tuck
on a manual machine like a Taitexma 160 or

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.000
an LK150 is actually worked as
a pull-up stitch, using using hold.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:52.640
Needles to tuck are pulled all the way out
and the carriage is set to hold position.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.280
It's the combination of the needle
positions and the hold setting

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.000
that carries the working yarn
over the needles in hold.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.360
After three rows, all needles
are knit, securing the tucks.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.520
The tucking needles are then alternated,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.760
creating this interesting
beautiful stitch pattern.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.360
For all machines,
here is the needle set up.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.200
One needle in work and two needles out

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.040
of work across the bed, leaving two
needles on each side in working position.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.600
This will secure the edge
stitches and create a selvedge.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.680
No tucks are formed on these end needles.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.240
On a manual machine,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.960
cast on and hang a cast on comb
or use a cast on rag and weights.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.880
Weight is important
for this stitch pattern.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:04.560
Let's identify the sets of needles
that will tuck as red and yellow.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.680
Start by pulling the red needles all

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.600
the way out and set your carriage
to hold in both directions.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.560
On this machine, put the levers to H.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.640
On an LK150, put the Russell
levers in this position.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.000
Tuck in both directions for three rows.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:42.680
Cancel hold and knit one row across

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.240
all the needles, securing the tucks.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.400
Next, pull the yellow
needles all the way out.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.800
Reset your carriage to hold in both
directions and knit three rows.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.080
Notice that you are tucking
over the opposite needles.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.240
Cancel hold and knit one row.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.400
That's it.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.480
It looks complicated, but these
eight rows are the entire pattern.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.240
Now, if you lose track of red and yellow
needles, watch your row counter.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.440
Row counter zero, one, and two,
hold the red needles.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.680
Row counter three, cancel hold.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.840
Row counters four, five, and six,
hold the yellow needles.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.280
Row counter seven, cancel hold.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.200
Create yourself a little cheat
sheet to help keep track.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.360
Another trick for keeping track
is to examine the knitting.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.320
Here I've just knit the closing
row and all the needles have knit.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.480
I can see the column of stitches here
that were knit and the tuck stitches here.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.200
This tells me that these are the needles
to pull for the next set of tucks.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.840
If you have a punch card or electronic

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.880
machine, the stitch
pattern looks like this.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.720
Instead of the hold setting,

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:15.880
like on a manual machine, we'll let the
automatic paterning select needles for us.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.760
On these machines,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.480
needles in working position will tuck
and needles in middle position will knit.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.880
On Brother machines, this is obvious.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.280
On Silver reed, this needle selection

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.160
happens behind the scenes
and it isn't as clear.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.040
This stitch pattern is
an eight-row repeat.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.320
The hardest part is determining what

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.360
needles should be in work and what
needles should be out of work.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.400
Remember, by default,
a punch card or electronic stitch pattern

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:53.880
will be aligned to the
center of the machine.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.880
So you need to determine
what zero is on your machine.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.480
For this stitch pattern, needle left one,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.200
the first needle to the left
of zero needs to be in work.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.080
The needle arrangement will
then be set up from there.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.800
One needle in work, two needles
completely out of work across the bed.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:34.400
To create a selvedge will keep
two needles in work on the edges.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.120
Let's stop here for a minute.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.360
The pattern asks for the first needle
to the left of zero to be in work.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.360
Now, maybe you've tried tuck lace before

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.840
and got stuck because you didn't know
what needle should be in and out of work.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.960
In the Brother Stitch World books,
the diagrams for tuck lace look like this.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.720
The O indicates the needles out of work.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.040
But where is zero?
It's never been clear to me.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.920
Plus, I discovered that my needle number

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.440
strip was not aligned to zero,
so I was putting the wrong needles out

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.800
of work when I first
tried this stitch pattern.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.760
Brother machines preselect needles,
and it's easy to dry knit this stitch

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.520
pattern and observe
which needles are tucking.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:30.040
The needles that are tucking are put
in B position or working position.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.680
Needles that are going to knit
are put in D or middle position.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.400
I used KC2 for this pattern

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:45.400
and ran my carriage
across to see which needles were tucking.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.120
And then set up my needle alignment.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:53.880
One in, two out, one in,
two out, all the way across.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:06.320
For Silver Singer machines,
one way to determine where your zero is is

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.400
to knit the stitch pattern
with all needles in work.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.520
Cast on with waste yarn and set your

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.800
machine to tuck and knit
the first row of the pattern.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.040
You'll immediately be able
to see which needles are tucking.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.320
Since your machine automatically centers
the stitch pattern on the needle bed,

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.560
the first tuck needle next
to your zero will be tucked.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.880
Based If on that selection, put

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.760
the rest of the needle arrangement
in place, one needle in, two needles out.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.800
One needle in, two needles out.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:46.080
For any automatic patterning machine,
once you've established the correct needle

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:52.120
arrangement, make sure you put those two
needles into working position as you knit.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:56.320
Your machine will want to tuck one
of those needles on the tuck rows.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.880
Pull them out each time so they knit

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.680
and you have a nice edge
to use for your garment.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.920
No matter what machine you use,

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.840
using weights and blocking are key
to this openwork stitch pattern.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.320
I suggest you let your knitting rest

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.360
and then use blocking wires
to secure the strands.

